 module-spec scc-module-specs site-set alias-site-set\nsrc/cljc/yang/core/publication.cljc:106:                binder-table refusal-ns publish-fn]\nsrc/cljc/yang/core/publication.cljc:138:                                    (emit1 :sites site-set)\nsrc/cljc/yang/core/publication.cljc:139:                                    (emit1 :binders binder-table)))\nsrc/cljc/yang/core/publication.cljc:142:                                  (refused (keyword refusal-ns \"site-set-unpublished\")\nsrc/cljc/yang/core/publication.cljc:162:                                                  (if alias-site-set\n1922:## 12. Safepoints\n429:(defn derive-envelope\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1922,2020p' docs/design/yang.php.md; sed -n '90,190p' src/cljc/yang/core/publication.cljc; sed -n '90,180p' src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc; sed -n '429,510p' src/cljc/yang/safepoint.cljc; rg -n 'binder|corpus|site' src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/{interpreter,pair}.cljc src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1922,2020p' docs/design/yang.php.md; sed -n '90,190p' src/cljc/yang/core/publication.cljc; sed -n '90,180p' src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc; sed -n '429,510p' src/cljc/yang/safepoint.cljc; rg -n 'binder|corpus|site' src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/{interpreter,pair}.cljc src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"## 12. Safepoints\n\n- `:loop` and `:call` hooks (`phpsp`) serve signals: `pcntl_signal` handlers\n  run at hooks via `stream/poll!` on a signal stream, and\n  `pcntl_async_signals` is the default and only mode. A recursion-depth bound\n  is available if a composition wants one (PHP has none; default off).\n- `max_execution_time` and `set_time_limit` are wall-clock in PHP and so\n  nondeterministic: the profile offers a deterministic **safepoint-count\n  budget** instead, raising the fatal with PHP's text, a recorded departure\n  (owner decision B8.5, corner C21). A composition may supply one non-negative\n  integer budget per run; omission means unlimited. Each entry to a `:loop` or\n  `:call` hook consumes one unit, and the hook after the last available unit\n  raises `PHP Fatal error:  Maximum execution time of 30 seconds exceeded`.\n  This operational fatal is not guest-catchable and skips shutdown functions\n  and destructors. It bounds execution between hooks, not wall-clock time or\n  blocking host operations; no generic VM instructions are charged.\n- **Statement ticks** (`declare(ticks=N)`, `register_tick_function`) use\n  selected `:line` sites after normal statement completion. The shared\n  `yang.safepoint` extension supports non-lambda statement sites and preserves\n  their values. P7-b must supply each site's lexical threshold and integrate\n  this mechanism with PHP's request-owned counter and callback registry.\n- No thread switches: PHP is single-threaded, and one request is one task.\n\n## 13. Diagnostics\n\nCompile time: `:yang.php/syntax`; `:yang.php/unsupported {:construct}`;\n`:yang.php/not-php`; `:yang.php/unknown-builtin` (a warning);\n`:yang.php/compile-error` for PHP's compile-time fatals (a function redeclared\nin one unit, `break 0`, a duplicate parameter, `$this` reassignment, a\npositional argument after a named one). Run time: guest warnings, notices and\ndeprecations as records on the guest-diagnostics stream; fatals as `Error`\nthrows; the uncaught-exception text pinned. Operational failures stay\noperational (`yang.antlr.md` section 11).\n\n## 14. The differential-corpus plan\n\n- **Oracle**: the pinned PHP 8.3.x CLI with the pinned ini. The comparison\n  covers stdout and the diagnostics stream, rendered in PHP's `Warning: ... in\n  <unit> on line N` form with the path replaced by the unit name and the line\n  derived from the side table (owner decision 2 of `yang.antlr.md` 8.5.1\n  reused), against PHP's stderr with paths normalized.\n- **Groups**: juggling (every pair of the comparison matrix, numeric strings,\n  arithmetic warnings and `TypeError`); arrays (key coercion, order, next\n  index after unset, copy-on-write observable through references, nested\n  writes, sort stability); references and lifecycle (section 4.6's three\n  sources); strings (bytes, interpolation forms, heredoc indentation,\n  `precision=14` output, `var_dump` text); functions (named arguments,\n  by-reference, defaults, variadics, coercion versus `strict_types`); classes\n  (trait precedence, visibility, late static binding, magic methods, readonly,\n  enums, `match`, destructors, `clone`); exceptions (`finally` ordering,\n  multi-catch, the `Error` hierarchy); generators; request (superglobals from\n  a fixture record, headers after output, `ob_*`); inclusion (`_once`, return\n  values, scope sharing, the two missing-include stages); safepoints (tick\n  counts, a signal at a hook).\n- **Fixture groups** are those of section 4.6, plus a request-record fixture\n  per corpus program. Corners are departure fixtures whose expected value is\n  the recorded deviation.\n\n## 15. Slice sequencing and acceptance contracts\n\nPHP depends on L1 to L4 (`yang.typescript.md` section 16) landing first. Every\nacceptance line runs bundled on four evaluators and linked on three until L-b,\non JVM, Node and Dart unless noted; source-level parsing is JVM-only. Every\nclaimed row's fixtures must equal the oracle before its slice lands (section\n17.3).\n\n+-------+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------+\n| Slice | Scope                            | Acceptance                        |\n+=======+==================================+===================================+\n| P0    | grammar package                  | packets for mixed HTML and code   |\n|       | `antlr/php/manifest.edn` with    | with byte spans; the `?>` newline |\n|       | the vendored MIT helper, worker  | rule preserved; span fixtures for |\n|       | profile, CST export of modes     | a heredoc with indentation        |\n|       | (inline HTML, heredoc,           | removal and interpolation, a      |\n|       | interpolation), licence block    | nowdoc, `?>` followed by a        |\n|       |                                  | newline and by text, `<?=`, and a |\n|       |                                  | close tag ending a statement      |\n|       |                                  | without `;`, each asserting the   |\n|       |                                  | exported spans cover exactly the  |\n|       |                                  | source and synthetic tokens       |\n|       |                                  | appear only where expected;       |\n|       |                                  | generics refused                  |\n+-------+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------+\n| P1    | core: `echo`, inline HTML, the   | the mixed text and code           |\n|       | output stack, scalars and the    | output-order fixture (exit        |\n|       | juggling table, operators with   | criterion 1); `echo 0.1+0.2`      |\n|       | overflow to float, byte strings, | prints `0.3` and                  |\n|       | control flow, functions with     | `var_dump(0.1+0.2)` prints        |\n|       | cells, the `php` prelude         | `float(0.30000000000000004)`;     |\n|       | skeleton with `init!`, bundled   | `PHP_INT_MAX + 1` is a float on   |\n|       | and linked profiles, the         | every host; float-address goldens |\n|       | `var_dump` renderer              | of the `php` prelude agree on     |\n|       |                                  | three hosts; `static` initializer |\n|       |                                  | timing; the `%` and `intdiv` edge |\n|       |                                  | fixtures; the comparison matrix;  |\n|       |                                  | `@` with a call-counting handler  |\n+-------+----------------------------------+-----------------------------------+\n| P2    | arrays: the value, key coercion, | the key-coercion fixture table;   |\n|       | nested writes, `foreach` by      | `$b = $a; $b[] = 1` leaves `$a`   |\n                    (components (mapv :name units) imports))})))\n\n\n(defn publish-units!\n  \"Publish `units` into the content store `handle` over the host registry\n   `registry`, the runtime module pinned at `runtime-address` and each\n   import outside the set at its address in `published`, in\n   `publication-order`, per the config `cfg` (see the namespace doc). Answers\n   `{:status :ok :addresses {name address} :bundles {bundle-symbol address}\n     :sites {name-or-bundle-symbol publication}\n     :binders {name-or-bundle-symbol publication}}`, or the first refusal:\n   `publication-order`'s before anything is written, else the publisher's\n   with a string `:unit`. An SCC member alias carries no marks; frontends\n   may publish its empty set with `:alias-site-set`.\"\n  [cfg handle registry runtime-address units published]\n  (let [{:keys [imported-names module-spec scc-module-specs site-set alias-site-set\n                binder-table refusal-ns publish-fn]\n         :or {publish-fn publish/publish-module!}}\n        cfg\n        plan (publication-order cfg units published)\n        by-name (into {} (map (juxt :name identity)) units)]\n    (if (= :refused (:status plan))\n      plan\n      (reduce\n        (fn [acc step]\n          (let [members (if (vector? step) step [step])\n                known (merge published (:addresses acc))\n                deps (select-keys known\n                                  (mapcat #(imported-names (get by-name %)) members))\n                specs (if (vector? step)\n                        (scc-module-specs registry (mapv by-name members)\n                                          runtime-address deps)\n                        [(module-spec registry (get by-name step)\n                                      runtime-address deps)])\n                bundle (first specs)\n                result (publish-fn handle bundle)\n                emit (fn [acc k r ast]\n                       ;; a handle with no byte store (a stub publisher)\n                       ;; has nowhere to put a set; a backend failure is\n                       ;; a refusal, not a throw\n                       (if-not (:put-bytes-fn handle)\n                         acc\n                         (try (let [emit1 (fn [acc slot f]\n                                            (if-let [pub (when f\n                                                           (f handle (:address r) ast))]\n                                              (assoc-in acc [slot k] pub)\n                                              acc))]\n                                (-> acc\n                                    (emit1 :sites site-set)\n                                    (emit1 :binders binder-table)))\n                              (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Exception :cljs :default) e\n                                (reduced\n                                  (refused (keyword refusal-ns \"site-set-unpublished\")\n                                           {:unit k,\n                                            :message (ex-message e)}))))))]\n            (if (= :refused (:status result))\n              (reduced (assoc result :unit (if (vector? step) (str (:name bundle)) step)))\n              (if (vector? step)\n                ;; A member alias holds no marks: the marks live in the\n                ;; bundle. Some frontends publish the alias's empty set too.\n                (let [base (-> acc\n                               (assoc-in [:bundles (:name bundle)] (:address result))\n                               (emit (:name bundle) result (:ast bundle)))]\n                  (if (reduced? base)\n                    base\n                    (let [aliases (reduce (fn [a [n spec]]\n                                            (let [r (publish-fn\n                                                      handle (assoc spec :requires\n                                                                    {(:name bundle) (:address result)}))]\n                                              (if (= :refused (:status r))\n                                                (reduced (assoc r :unit n))\n                                                (let [a (assoc-in a [:addresses n] (:address r))]\n                                                  (if alias-site-set\n                                                    (emit a n r (:ast spec))\n                                                    a)))))\n                                          base\n                                          (map vector members (rest specs)))]\n                      (if (= :refused (:status aliases)) (reduced aliases) aliases))))\n                (-> acc\n                    (assoc-in [:addresses step] (:address result))\n                    (emit step result (:ast bundle)))))))\n        {:status :ok, :addresses {}, :bundles {}}\n        (:order plan)))))\n(def code-formats\n  \"The four code format records a module manifest links through.\"\n  [linker/ast-format linker/semantic-format\n   linker/stack-format linker/register-format])\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Call-site occurrence contract C1 (Addendum A, design §2)\n;; =============================================================================\n;;\n;; A module whose frontend finalized its tree (`:yang/call-site 1` on\n;; every final `:application` occurrence) publishes its frontend-metadata\n;; side table beside its manifest: the manifest keeps the closed\n;; schema-1 key set (a manifest address names code, and nothing else),\n;; so the side table never enters the hashed manifest. The manifest is\n;; materialized first, and the publication record then names it\n;; (`:yang.call-site/manifest`), beside the tree root, the publication's\n;; recorded admission origin, and the table's address — a record names\n;; a manifest, never the reverse (the site-set precedent,\n;; `yang.python.antlr.siteset`). Custody of the record and table runs\n;; through the `publish-module!` result's `:call-sites`, not through\n;; the module closure: execution never consults C1 tables, so the\n;; closure walker does not follow them. The publication's origin is an ADMISSION identity\n;; (design §2.1): a token the composition pins explicitly, draws from\n;; a composition-owned scope object, or — by default — has derived\n;; from the publication specification's own durable identity; no\n;; counter or other mutable state lives in this namespace. The token\n;; is persisted in the record and retrieved from the record, never\n;; reconstructed from the tree's content. The publisher\n;; extracts the markers from the map AST, refuses the\n;; publication when they do not exactly cover the canonical tree's\n;; application occurrences — or when a marker's value is not version 1 —\n;; and otherwise materializes the table and its record into the same\n;; handle. A module with no markers makes no claim and publishes exactly\n;; as before.\n\n(defn admission-scope\n  \"An admission scope for `publish-module!`'s `:yin.link.publish/scope`\n   option. `id` names this publisher — a durable, caller-chosen\n   identity (a string or keyword) that distinguishes this publisher's\n   admissions from every other publisher's; a publisher that must not\n   collide with its own past runs takes a fresh `id` per run. The\n   returned scope object OWNS the publication ordinal counter, so no\n   counter lives in this namespace (no hidden global state): two\n   publications through one scope draw consecutive ordinals, and two\n   independent scopes publishing identical specifications still yield\n   distinct admission identities, because their `id`s differ.\"\n  [id]\n  {:yin.link.publish/scope-id id\n   :yin.link.publish/ordinal (atom 0)})\n\n(defn- scope-admission-token\n  \"The admission token a scope object draws for one publication: the\n   scope's own `id` plus the ordinal the scope's counter assigns. The\n   counter lives in the caller-owned scope object, never here.\"\n  [{:yin.link.publish/keys [scope-id ordinal]}]\n  ;; pr-str keeps the value's full identity — a keyword's namespace, a\n  ;; string's quoting, a symbol's name — so distinct caller-chosen ids\n  ;; (:a/publisher, :b/publisher, \"publisher\") never mint the same token\n  (keyword \"yin.vm.linker.publish.scope\"\n           (str (pr-str scope-id) \"#\" (swap! ordinal inc))))\n\n(defn- spec-admission-token\n  \"The default admission token: derived deterministically from the\n   publication specification's own durable identity — the module name\n   and the content address of its compiled tree — never from hidden\n   mutable state. Two publications of the same specification therefore\n   answer the same admission identity (an idempotent re-publication is\n   the same recorded admission), and distinct specifications never\n   share one, in this or any other publisher process. A composition\n   that admits the same specification more than once as separate\n   admissions passes an `:yin.link.publish/scope` (or an explicit\n   `:yin.link.publish/admission`) so the admissions stay distinct.\"\n  [{:keys [name]} tree]\n  (keyword \"yin.vm.linker.publish.publication\"\n           (str name \"::\" (:root tree))))\n\n(defn- publication-origin\n  \"The occurrence origin of the publication admission `token` names:\n   the publisher's own medium, the explicit admission token, member 0.\n   `token` is the publication's admission identity — the\n   `:yin.link.publish/admission` option when the composition pins one,\n   the `:yin.link.publish/scope` option's next drawn token when the\n   composition scopes the publisher, otherwise the token derived from\n   the publication specification's own durable identity. The origin is\n   an admission event's identity, not a function of the tree's content\n   alone: it is persisted in the side record at publication time and\n   retrieved from that record; nothing ever reconstructs it from the\n   root.\"\n  [token]\n  [:source :yin.vm.linker.publish/publish token 0])\n(defn derive-envelope\n  \"One projected envelope from the row medium to the derived one:\n   `{:envelope e' :record r :record-address a}`. The run member of the input\n   is `A`; `e'` carries `A'` alone, on `medium`, with token `a`.\n\n   When the input envelope's frontend-metadata claims C1 for the run\n   member, `e'` carries the recomputed rows of `A'` and the answer\n   carries `:correspondence`, the explicit input→output occurrence\n   correspondence (also on `e'` as `:yang.safepoint/correspondence`).\n   `opts` may supply `:injected-origin`, the caller's admission origin\n   for the injected hook applications; without one they stay\n   unclaimed. No origin is ever derived from the trees' structure.\"\n  ([envelope profile medium]\n   (derive-envelope envelope profile medium nil))\n  ([envelope profile medium {:keys [injected-origin]}]\n   (when (and injected-origin (not (vm/occurrence-origin? injected-origin)))\n     (throw (ex-info \"Malformed injected call-site origin\"\n                     {:rule :origin, :origin injected-origin})))\n   (let [{:yin/keys [source-medium batch-token batch root frontend-metadata]}\n         envelope\n         tree (nth batch root)\n         origin (vm/source-origin source-medium batch-token root)\n         input-sites (sites frontend-metadata origin (:root tree))\n         {derived :tree selected :sites}\n         (insert tree input-sites profile)\n         {:keys [metadata correspondence]}\n         (derive-call-sites frontend-metadata tree derived\n                            (selected-hooks input-sites profile)\n                            injected-origin)\n         record (derive-record (:root tree) (:root derived) profile selected)\n         address (jing/segment-key record)]\n     (vm/source-origin medium address 0)\n     (cond-> {:envelope (cond-> {:yin/source-medium medium,\n                                 :yin/batch-token address,\n                                 :yin/batch [derived],\n                                 :yin/root 0,\n                                 :yin/source-positions [],\n                                 :yin/frontend-metadata (or metadata []),\n                                 :yin/entity-occurrences []}\n                          correspondence\n                          (assoc :yang.safepoint/correspondence correspondence)),\n              :record record,\n              :record-address address}\n       correspondence (assoc :correspondence correspondence)))))\n\n\n(defn transform\n  \"The stage's transform over projected envelopes: `A'` on port `:program`,\n   its `:derive` record on port `:ledger`. State is `{:profile p :medium m}`\n   and never changes.\"\n  [{:keys [profile medium], :as state} envelope]\n  (let [{:keys [envelope record]} (derive-envelope envelope profile medium)]\n    [state [[:program envelope] [:ledger record]]]))\n\n\n(defn open-stage\n  \"A safepoint stage reading projected envelopes from `rows`, writing derived\n   envelopes to `program` (logical identity `medium`) and records to\n   `ledger`.\"\n  [rows program ledger profile medium]\n  (stage/open rows\n              {:program program, :ledger ledger}\n              {:profile profile, :medium medium}))\n\n\n(defn step-stage\n  [s]\n  (stage/step s transform))\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:1:(ns yang.python.antlr.siteset\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:2:  \"C4 slice I7 (docs/design/yang.antlr.md 8.5.6, ruling 14): published site\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:7:   site set is that side table's `:yang/site` marks for the module tree,\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:8:   sorted (`yang.safepoint/site-set`) and content-addressed. A separate\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:16:   `{:yang.site/manifest m :yang.site/record record-address ...}`, and\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:17:   indexes it as the datoms `[e :yang.site/envelope env]` and `[e\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:18:   :yang.site/proof proof]` beside its name envelopes (`signed-entry`).\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:44:  \"yang.python.antlr.lower/sites-1\")\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:50:  {:yang.site/unit :module,\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:51:   :yang.site/prelude :linked,\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:52:   :yang.site/kinds [:call :line :loop]})\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:62:  :yang.python.antlr/site-set)\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:65:(defn site-set-of\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:68:   sorted site set.\"\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:71:                               (medium) [:site-set 0] [ast]))\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:75:        sites (safepoint/sites (:yin/frontend-metadata env) origin (:root tree))]\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:76:    {:root (:root tree), :tree tree, :set (safepoint/site-set sites)}))\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:80:  \"The publication record associating manifest `manifest` with site set\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:83:  {:yang.site/manifest manifest,\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:84:   :yang.site/root root,\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:85:   :yang.site/revision frontend-revision,\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:86:   :yang.site/profile profile-address,\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:87:   :yang.site/set set-address})\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:91:  \"Publish the site set of module `ast` (published at manifest address\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:95:  (let [{:keys [root set]} (site-set-of ast)\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:150:  (let [env {:yang.site/op :publish,\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:151:             :yang.site/manifest manifest,\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:152:             :yang.site/record record-address,\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:161:                 [[e :yang.site/envelope envelope]\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:162:                  [e :yang.site/proof proof]])\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:174:                           (= a :yang.site/proof) (update e (fnil conj #{}) v)))\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:178:                   (when (and (= a :yang.site/envelope)\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:180:                              (= :publish (:yang.site/op env))\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:181:                              (= manifest (:yang.site/manifest env)))\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:189:                         (:yang.site/record env)))))\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:216:                        (:yang.site/kinds lowering-profile))))))\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:220:  \"Nil when the record at `record-address` verifies as the site set of the\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:232:        set-address (when (map? r) (:yang.site/set r))\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:238:                 (not= manifest (:yang.site/manifest r)) {:defect :manifest}\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:239:                 (not= (:yin.module/tree m) (:yang.site/root r)) {:defect :root}\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:240:                 (not= frontend-revision (:yang.site/revision r))\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:242:                 (not= profile-address (:yang.site/profile r)) {:defect :profile}\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:271:                \" is noninterruptible: imported without a site set\")}\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:275:      (refusal :yang.python.antlr/missing-site-set requirement\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:276:               \"no published site set for the module\" {:manifest manifest})\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:283:              (let [set-address (:yang.site/set (fetch handle a))]\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:294:            (refusal :yang.python.antlr/site-set-mismatch requirement\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:295:                     (str \"the published site set\"\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:342:   hooks of `(:hooks-profile requirement)` at its sites, requiring\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:359:              sites (into {} (:set adm))\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:361:                         (safepoint/insert tree sites\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:400:                             (:sites inserted)))}))\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:496:  #{:yang.python.antlr/missing-site-set\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:497:    :yang.python.antlr/site-set-mismatch\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:508:   call dispatcher, not guest Python code, so it has no Python site marks.\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:542:  \"The `publisher` key's signed publication entries for the site sets\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:543:   `publish-units!` answered: `sites` (`:sites`), the manifest of each\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:548:  [publisher sites addresses bundles]\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/siteset.cljc:552:        (sort-by (comp str key) sites)))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
